gthr-posix: use rtoskit-libc's pthreads as a static library provides them

libgcc and libstdc++ reach threads only through gthr.h, and --enable-threads=posix
selects gthr-posix.h, which fits rtoskit-libc's <pthread.h> but for two things that
come from the library being linked statically on a bare-metal core.

__gthread_active_p. Outside glibc and Bionic, gthr-posix.h decides that threads
are active from a weak reference to pthread_cancel. In a static link a weak
reference pulls no archive member, and rtoskit-libc has no pthread_cancel, so every
mutex in a program would be a no-op. libstdc++'s os/generic port already turns the
weak references off for libstdc++ (_GLIBCXX_GTHREAD_USE_WEAK 0); libgcc's copy of
the header has no such port, so when the C library installs <rtoskit-libc.h>
(__RTOSKIT_LIBC__) the header turns them off itself, which makes every pthread call
a strong reference and __gthread_active_p constant 1.

__gthread_once. pthread_once is a C function with no unwind tables, so an exception
from the function of a std::call_once would terminate the program and leave the flag
set. rtoskit-libc's __libc_call_once_eh does the same on the same flag (its
pthread_once_t has once_flag's layout), is built with exception tables, runs the
function under a cleanup that resets the flag, and so lets the exception travel and
the next std::call_once run the function again. __gthread_once calls it under
__RTOSKIT_LIBC__. It is declared here with an assembler label, so the header needs
none of the library's headers beyond <pthread.h>.

The patch applies to GCC 14, 15 and 16. Nothing else is needed for the posix
model: libstdc++ finds pthread_cond_clockwait and pthread_mutex_clocklock with its
own compile tests, so steady_clock waits use CLOCK_MONOTONIC, not a converted
deadline.

diff -ruN a/libgcc/gthr-posix.h b/libgcc/gthr-posix.h
--- a/libgcc/gthr-posix.h
+++ b/libgcc/gthr-posix.h
@@ -35,4 +35,32 @@
 #include <pthread.h>
 
+/* A C library that provides the pthreads for a static program says so with
+   <rtoskit-libc.h>.  */
+#if defined __has_include
+# if __has_include(<rtoskit-libc.h>)
+#  include <rtoskit-libc.h>
+# endif
+#endif
+
+#ifdef __RTOSKIT_LIBC__
+/* A static link resolves no weak reference to a function that no object pulls
+   in, so the weak pthread_cancel probe in __gthread_active_p would find the
+   threads inactive.  Every call is a strong reference instead.  */
+# undef GTHREAD_USE_WEAK
+# define GTHREAD_USE_WEAK 0
+
+/* pthread_once on the same flag, built with exception tables: an exception from
+   the function leaves the flag unset.  Declared by its label, so that no
+   header of the library but <pthread.h> is needed here; once_flag and
+   pthread_once_t have the same layout.  */
+# ifdef __cplusplus
+extern "C"
+# else
+extern
+# endif
+void __gthread_rtoskit_once (pthread_once_t *, void (*) (void))
+  __asm__ ("__libc_call_once_eh");
+#endif
+
 #if ((defined(_LIBOBJC) || defined(_LIBOBJC_WEAK)) \
      || !defined(_GTHREAD_USE_MUTEX_TIMEDLOCK))
@@ -743,8 +771,13 @@
 __gthread_once (__gthread_once_t *__once, void (*__func) (void))
 {
+#ifdef __RTOSKIT_LIBC__
+  __gthread_rtoskit_once (__once, __func);
+  return 0;
+#else
   if (__gthread_active_p ())
     return __gthrw_(pthread_once) (__once, __func);
   else
     return -1;
+#endif
 }
 
